<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary, mother of Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE – 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Virgin Mary, Blessed Virgin Mary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, known as the Virgin Mary, is venerated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, above all in the Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches. Medieval rulers dedicated lands to her: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> dedicated conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> to her, giving Terra Mariana its name, and the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> declared he held his town as her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> alone.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus.jpg" alt="Mary, mother of Jesus" width="320" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madonna_Advocata.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It taught salvation by grace through faith and the works of faith, not by faith alone; it reaffirmed transubstantiation and the seven sacraments; and it strongly reaffirmed pilgrimages, the veneration of saints, relics and images, and the veneration of the <b>Virgin Mary</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Parthenon then became a church of the Parthenos Maria, the <b>Virgin Mary</b>, its entrance moved to the west and an apse built at the east.</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Ave maris stella&quot; (&quot;Hail, star of the sea&quot;) is a medieval Latin hymn to the <b>Virgin Mary</b>, usually sung at Vespers.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a></li><li><blockquote>The first stanza calls on the <b>Virgin Mary</b>, the chosen mother of Christ, to win her Son's favour for the people; the second turns to Christ himself, recalling his baptism, and asks for a good life on earth and life in heaven after death.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>Every song mentions the <b>Virgin Mary</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)</a></li><li><blockquote>The prophecy had featured in Christian hymns since at least the 8th century, when Cosmas the Melodist wrote of the <b>Virgin Mary</b> flowering from the root of Jesse.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li></ul></section>
